  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A storm tore open a hillside and exposed stone houses. Winter, 1850. The Bay of Skaill, on Orkney, off the north coast of Scotland.",
        "visual_query": "orkney coastal storm waves",
        "visual_fallback": "stormy coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Ten houses. Low doorways joined by covered passages. Inside: stone beds, stone dressers, stone storage boxes, and drains running under the floors.",
        "visual_query": "neolithic stone dwelling interior",
        "visual_fallback": "stone ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Radiocarbon puts people here from about 3180 BC, living on to around 2500 BC. Older than the Great Pyramid. So why is any of it still standing?",
        "visual_query": "orkney islands green coastline",
        "visual_fallback": "island coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Sand. The village was buried gradually and sealed airtight, which saved things that should have rotted \u2014 bone needles, ivory pins, even twisted heather rope.",
        "visual_query": "windswept sand dunes coast",
        "visual_fallback": "sand dunes"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Around 2500 BC the climate turned colder and wetter and they went. Some appear to have walked out and left prized possessions sitting where they were.",
        "visual_query": "scottish islands grey sea",
        "visual_fallback": "grey sea"
      }
    ],
